{
"dur": 71.336,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 14.319,
"text": "Imagine eBay and TikTok had a baby. You scroll through endless live streams, and in each one somebody is selling something in real time. Trainers, coins, toys, designer bags. And, above all, trading cards.",
"end": 14.594
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 14.594,
"vo_start": 14.869,
"vo_end": 25.829,
"text": "Anyone can sell. You open a shop, go live, and Whatnot takes a cut of up to eight per cent. It runs in America, Canada, Australia, several EU countries, and here in the UK.",
"end": 26.104
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 26.104,
"vo_start": 26.379,
"vo_end": 35.458,
"text": "But the real action is something called a box break. A seller opens a sealed box of cards live on camera, and viewers buy a spot: a share of whatever comes out.",
"end": 35.733
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 35.733,
"vo_start": 36.008,
"vo_end": 48.448,
"text": "In a randomised break, you don't choose your team. You pay first, and then a randomiser hands you one. Good team, great cards. Bad team, bad luck. Sometimes your team isn't even in the box.",
"end": 48.723
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 48.723,
"vo_start": 48.998,
"vo_end": 59.998,
"text": "Mystery repacks work the same way. You buy a pack without knowing what's inside, or what it's worth. The host rips it open, the chat goes wild, and somewhere, someone wins big.",
"end": 60.273
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 60.273,
"vo_start": 60.548,
"vo_end": 70.386,
"text": "Whatnot says every buyer must get at least one card per break, and anyone sent an empty envelope gets a refund. But remember one word: chance. This whole story hangs on it.",
"end": 71.336
}
],
